ed Portfolio 40.06% (5yr). The Pension Switch Report (P7) references charge comparisons and benefits analysis but quoted passages do not provide the specific numerical cost comparison or aggregated impact. File notes (P10) record current risk ratings: Aviva 2, Zurich 7, Standard Life 4. The research report confirms performance data is calculated 'with dividends/distributions reinvested, including the effect of ongoing costs and charges.' No passage directly quantifies the cost saving claimed in the report or demonstrates that lower costs justify the switch when Zurich funds have outperformed the recommendation.","discrepancies":["Report claims 'lower overall cost structure' but no evidence passage quantifies this cost advantage numerically.","Report states recommended funds 'have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings' (true per P4) but then claims this supports the switch, yet also acknowledges they 'have not outperformed your current Zurich funds' (P4), which are the higher-performing existing holding.","Report asserts benefits including adviser charging facilitation and ongoing monitoring as positives, but these are service-level benefits, not cost savings, and their cost is not quantified against alternatives.","Report emphasizes 'Greater Investment Choice' as a benefit (6,900+ funds on Aviva), but case file (P8, P10) shows existing schemes also offer substantial choice: Aviva existing offers max 50 funds at one time, Standard Life and Zurich also offer multiple funds. No evidence that the additional choice materially improves outcomes for this client's stated objectives.","Report claims 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' and 'tax-efficient withdrawal strategies' but no specific evidence in the file quantifies how this flexibility delivers measurable client value relative to existing schemes' flexibility."],"comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Quantified cost comparison table showing initial costs, ongoing charges (platform, fund, advice), and cumulative cost impact across planning horizon for each existing scheme versus recommended scheme.","Explicit cost figure for adviser charges and how these are charged on existing schemes versus new scheme (directly deductible on Aviva versus otherwise).","Aggregated all-in cost as percentage or pounds for each option to enable fair comparison.","Evidence that the cited benefits (adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring, retirement flexibility) either reduce cost or materially improve outcomes, with supporting data.","Quantified impact of switching away from the best-performing existing holding (Zurich at 44.32% over 5 years versus recommended Aviva at 40.02%), showing how other benefits offset this performance deficit."],"analysis":"The report presents a benefits case but fails to ground the central assertion—'Lower Costs'—in quantified evidence. P4 shows that the recommended Aviva funds (40.02% cumulative 5-year performance) underperformed the client's existing Zurich holding (44.32%), which itself has higher volatility (7.89% vs 7.56%). The report acknowledges this underperformance but does not demonstrate that a cost saving is large enough to justify moving from the better-performing scheme. The report lists six benefits, but most are qualitative and service-oriented (adviser charging facilitation, monitoring, flexibility) rather than quantified cost or return improvements. The 'Greater Investment Choice' benefit is not evidenced: existing schemes offer substantial fund counts, and no evidence shows the Aviva choice set is materially superior for this client's objectives. The core rhetorical move—asserting 'lower costs' leads to 'more savings remain invested for growth'—is undermined by the fact that the recommended scheme has delivered lower growth (40.02% vs 44.32%) despite claimed lower costs. Without a quantified cost comparison (P7 references a Pension Switch Report with charge comparisons, but the quoted passages do not provide the numerical basis), the assertion of cost benefit cannot be verified. PRIN 2A.4 requires fair value; where a recommendation increases costs or reduces net benefit, the burden is on the adviser to demonstrate the trade-off is justified. The report does not meet this standard: it asserts lower costs without evidence, acknowledges lower performance, and relies on unquantified service benefits that may not offset either the cost or the performance gap.","citations":[{"passageId":"P4","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The recommended Aviva advised funds have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings. However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds."},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Aviva Risk 5 portfolio TR in GB | 40.02 | Zurich - Managed Pn AP GTR in GB | 44.32"},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges."},{"passageId":"P2","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"The table shows an investment's performance, calculated as NAV to NAV/Bid to Bid/Closing Price to Closing Price, with dividends/distributions reinvested, including the effect of ongoing costs and charges."},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"New solution charges comparison"}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

